2026-09-15T20:31Z claude note Filed from the author's ruling of this date, quoted verbatim in the body. It SUPERSEDES the "Deferred: the on/off control" section of closed ticket 0792, which recorded the analysis and explicitly scheduled nothing: the widget is now decided (a checkbox), and so is the label (a state, "Pause indexing", not an action). Two of 0792's own conclusions are corrected by the ruling rather than confirmed, and both corrections are written out below so the next agent does not re-derive them from a closed ticket: Pause/Play was rejected there and is now the author's chosen word, which turns "extraction cannot be paused once a file has started" from a veto into a design constraint on one string; and 0792's recommended role="switch" should be DROPPED, because a control labelled "Pause indexing" is a checkbox and saying so is free. One clause of the ruling admits two readings and the body names which one is adopted, why, and the single paragraph that changes if the author meant the other.
2026-09-15T20:34Z claude note Cross-session input from the concurrent sitter session (padme, same repo, author-authorised): the sync condition this ticket needs in Action 4 has a candidate reading, Zotero.Sync.Runner.syncInProgress, and a warning that comes with it -- it must be sampled TWICE, once during an active sync and once at rest, because a key that reads the same in both states does not discriminate and must not be used. Folded into Action 4 rather than left in a chat log. That session also holds PR #585, which bumps the sitter manifest to 0.4.17; THIS PR is tickets-only and touches no plugin file, so the implementation of this ticket takes 0.4.18 or later. Two live findings from that session bear on the control's value and are recorded here because they are the reason the author is tired of the button: toggling off/on is currently the only way to break the unbounded drain loop (stop() burns the epoch, start() sets reconciliationPending, forcing a fresh census), and a fresh census is itself needed because ~13 700 attachments enumerated while their files were not yet on disk are classed missing-source, which is in `failed` and not in `queued`. Incident dossier: verification/incidents/0795-2026-09-15-drain-during-sync.md, tickets 0795 and 0796.
2026-09-15T20:36Z claude note CORRECTION to this ticket's 20:34Z entry, which is append-only and therefore stands wrong above: toggling off/on is NOT the escape hatch from ticket 0796's drain starvation. The concurrent sitter session measured it from `sdt-sitter-last-shutdown.json` across two real off/on cycles (20:16:55 and 20:22:53) and the record splits in two. What the toggle DOES do, and what this ticket's invariant now protects, is force a fresh census within two seconds of each `on`, and that census works: `failed` fell 13 749 -> 5 242 and `pending` rose 436 -> 8 926 as attachments whose files had landed left `missing-source`. What it does NOT do is restart the pump: each census was followed by a `drain-start` of ~9 600 dirty ids, after which `pending` froze and `active` stayed null, seven minutes the second time, and BOTH `sweep-end` records carry `completed: 0` -- 8 926 admissible candidates, not one submitted, nothing indexed all session. The starvation is also not permanent: between 20:08 and 20:15 `pending` rose 44 -> 389, which only happens if `refreshQueue()` ran, so the drain had emptied when the notification source dried up. Dossier: verification/incidents/0796-2026-09-15-toggle-does-not-recover.md. Two attributions in that session's reading are corrected here: the toggles and the 20:31:54Z uninstall were NOT this session's doing -- this session has no console on the live Zotero instance and touched neither -- so whoever took those actions is unidentified by anything either session measured, and the incident record should not name one.
2026-09-15T20:40Z claude note CHECKED, not relayed: PR #585's exclusion of `no-attachment` from the "Not indexed" heading does NOT propagate into this control's own sentence, and the effect on this ticket is the opposite of a hazard. Read the two routes rather than taking the concurrent session's warning on trust. The switch line's exception figure is `coverage.unindexed + coverage.failed`, tallied by `getSDTCoverage` over `SDT_STATUS_CLASSES` -- `unindexed: ['empty-pack']`, `failed: ['failed-session', 'inspection-error', 'unsupported-pack', 'missing-source']` (scheduler.js:13-31). `no-attachment` appears in none of those keys: it is not a status at all but a separate list, `state.unattached`, filled by `host.unattached()` (scheduler.js:215-216), which scheduler.js:213 itself calls "the separate no-attachment view". The heading is the other route, `groups.reduce(...members.length)` over `collectSDTNotIndexed` (bootstrap.js:1466), and that is the one #585 changes -- verified against the PR's own diff, not its description. So the sentence never counted `no-attachment`, the heading did, and the two have been disagreeing all along while `switch-on-exceptions` told the reader to compare them ("{count} attachments are not indexed -- see 'Not indexed' below"). #585 CLOSES that divergence. The consequence for this ticket is a thing not to undo: an implementer rewriting `describeSDTIdleLine` must not reintroduce it, which is why it is now an invariant below.
2026-09-15T20:42Z claude note AUTHOR RULED, and with a third reading that discards the mechanism the first two were arguing about: "Je prefere ne pas avoir d'etat donc aucune preference memorisee. D'ailleurs ce n'est marque nulle part 'Preference memorisee'. On commence allume des que possible." So no remembered answer at all -- the box is checked and inert while indexing cannot be launched, and the moment it can be, indexing STARTS, unasked; checking the box pauses for this session only. Body rewritten accordingly: the adopted/not-adopted pair is gone, Action 3b removes the startup pref read AND askSDTLaunch together (half of it is worse than none -- dropping the modal while still reading the pref leaves a machine obeying an answer the user can no longer give), and the persistence invariant is replaced. His second sentence was checked rather than accepted: exactly one string claims persistence, `launch-details` ("This answer is remembered."), and it lives in the modal shown at most once per profile and deliberately never seen again, so the only statement of the memory sits on the one surface designed never to return while the window he actually consults says nothing -- which argues for the ruling. THE CONSEQUENCE HE HAS NOT YET SEEN, and it is why Action 9 exists: SPEC.md R22 requires in as many words that the stop "MUST hold across restarts", with the rationale that work resuming after a reboot "was never stopped, only interrupted". A session-scoped pause contradicts that clause. It is his requirement and his to change, but the amendment must land in the same PR as the behaviour -- a SPEC that claims the opposite of the running code is worse than either state. Also recorded, from the same message: the 20:31:54Z uninstall and the two toggles were the author's own, so nothing is owed to the 0796 incident record on attribution.
2026-09-15T20:45Z claude note Two mechanics of the version guard folded into Action 8, from the concurrent session that hit both, and confirmed against bench/check_sitter_version.py rather than relayed. It digests the delivered payload with hashlib.sha256 and holds that no two DIFFERENT payloads share a version, so a comment-only edit reddens it -- that session's added comment did, under 0.4.17, and it amended its commit rather than take 0.4.18. And its forge leg answers NOT-RUN, on its own line, when it cannot enumerate the open PRs (an HTTP 504 did it once), because "a gate that cannot look must never answer as" green -- the script says so at line 85. In that state a version another open branch has already claimed reads as unclaimed, so a NOT-RUN must be re-run and never counted as a pass. Same shape as this ticket's other two controls, the sync probe and the blocked-phase test arm: an all-clear indistinguishable from "I could not look" is not a check, and this guard is unusual in saying which one it is giving you. Also noted: ticket 0795's Action 1 now carries the sync probe's availability constraint in writing -- neither arm is available on demand, the reading needs a live Zotero window with the Run JavaScript panel, and the active arm needs a real bulk sync that must be caught rather than provoked, since while 0796 is unfixed such a sync starves the sitter for its whole duration.
2026-09-15T20:47Z claude note AUTHOR ON R22: "Zotero a un bouton pour desactiver un plugin." That resolves the conflict better than the amendment this ticket proposed an hour ago, and Action 9 is rewritten accordingly: R22 is NOT weakened. Its two clauses are answered by two controls, one of them the host's -- the checkbox pauses for the session, Zotero's own add-on disable is the stop that holds across restarts, by construction, and re-implementing a durable stop beside it was the plugin storing state the host already stores. What SPEC.md owes is a sentence saying which control answers which clause, since the text today reads as though one switch owed both. CHECKED, and the check changes what the SPEC edit must say: ticket 0742 -- titled "Collapse the sitter's launch prompt and pause into one persisted switch" -- considered add-on disable for exactly this role and rejected it, and bootstrap.js:1039-1042 still carries the reasons, that it "held across restarts but lived four clicks away in Tools -> Add-ons and removed the very window that would have shown the sitter stopped". Both are still true; what changed is their weight, and the edit must carry them forward rather than drop them: they were fatal while disable was the only durable stop and the routine need had to travel through it, and a rare deliberate act may cost four clicks and may take the window with it. This ticket therefore REVERSES 0742 -- its launch prompt goes, its persistence goes, the durable half returns to the host -- and what survives is one control in the window, with paused a state the user can see and leave. Action 9 also now requires updating the bootstrap.js:1038 comment block, which opens "the switch: R22's one obvious way (ticket 0742)" and would otherwise describe a design that no longer exists.
2026-09-15T20:48Z claude note ACTION 3b WAS WRONG and is corrected, on three readings from the concurrent session, each confirmed in the source here rather than relayed. (1) "askSDTLaunch and its five strings" was wrong twice over: the modal owns SIX ids, and `launch-worker`/`launch-disable` are not among them -- bootstrap.js:2019 feeds those two, with `about-updates`, to the About panel's disclosures. They are precisely the paragraphs saying that extraction runs on a shared background process and that turning indexing off lets the file in flight finish, which is what a user who has just ticked "Pause indexing" goes looking for, so a `launch-` prefix sweep would empty About of the text this ticket's own label makes relevant. (2) `writeSDTSwitch` also calls `emit('switch', { enabled })` at bootstrap.js:1065, and those ring records are how this evening's two cycles and their `completed: 0` were reconstructed at all -- the emission must survive the pref write's removal or the control stops leaving any trace. (3) `readSDTSwitch`'s `null` is not only "never answered": the catch returns it for an UNREADABLE pref too, deliberately, per bootstrap.js:1049-1051 -- silently indexing on a machine whose answer could not be read is what the question exists to prevent. So the tri-state is a safety guard as well as a memory, and starting on unconditionally dissolves it. Under this ruling it has nothing left to protect, since indexing unasked is now the intended default, but that must be decided in the commit rather than carried away. Action 9 also gains R22's second citation site, removeSDTDurableState at bootstrap.js:3628-3630, whose comment contrasts the debug pref with "R22's tri-state discipline for ENABLED_PREF above" and would otherwise point at a retired clause.
2026-09-15T20:53Z claude note RETRACTION, of a claim this ticket's own 20:36Z entry carries and which is now known false. That entry ends "The starvation is also not permanent: between 20:08 and 20:15 `pending` rose 44 -> 389, which only happens if `refreshQueue()` ran, so the drain had emptied when the notification source dried up." The inference is invalid and the conclusion is withdrawn. `refreshQueue()` is NOT reachable only from the reconciliation at scheduler.js:176: `record()` calls it at line 113, and the drain calls `record()` at line 210 on every turn -- verified in the source here, not taken on report. So `state.pending` is rebuilt continuously throughout the drain, a rise in it says nothing about the drain having emptied, and 44 was a live value rather than a frozen snapshot -- it stayed at 44 because nothing was changing class, `missing-source` being in `failed` and not in `queued`. The concurrent session found this by checking its own citations rather than its prose, and retracted it upstream; 0796's recovery question is REOPENED, and its title is tightened to "starves admission". What survives is measured rather than inferred, and this ticket leans on none of the retracted part: the unbounded loop at scheduler.js:201, admission never reached, `active: null`, `completed: 0` across two sweeps, no `drain-end`. Nothing in this ticket's body or invariants rests on "the sitter recovers by itself" -- checked before writing this, the phrase and the claim appear nowhere outside that log entry -- so the correction costs this ticket one retraction and no edit.

