2026-09-16T07:55Z claude note TWO CORRECTIONS TO THIS TICKET'S OWN BODY, both made in the body above rather than only here. (1) native-worker-busy is NOT in SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES -- verified on origin/main at e0cd54f3 -- so the 'Which states check the box by themselves' paragraph listed a sixth member that does not exist, and the table's 'a blocked phase' row did not cover it. It is a WAIT, not a block: nextSweepDelayMS gives it the ordinary sweep interval instead of the ten-minute idle one, and scheduler.js emits it at trace as worker-idle-wait rather than as a refuse. It lands on the unchecked, interactive row anyway, for that row's own reasons -- the user has paused nothing, it is transient, and it already has a decided SDT_PHASE_LABELS entry -- and it gets there by falling through, named nowhere. The general form: two classifications agreeing on five of six members are the ones that get conflated, and SDT_SYNC_PHASE is the same trap in the other direction, so neither membership nor non-membership decides this table. (2) The in-flight row's promise, 'finishing the current document, then stopping', is false: a submitted extraction cannot be ended from this side at all, and from here a slow tail and a wedged worker leave the same journal (DESIGN-NOTES open question 4, ticket 0793). What ships is switch-off-in-flight, which says the assistant has no way to end it and names Zotero's Add-ons window as the stop that works -- R22's durable clause doing the work its own ruling assigns it.
2026-09-16T07:55Z claude note PHASE AGE, the author's ask of 2026-09-15, shipped as a render-local observation and not as a scheduler field. Each dialog notes when it first saw the current announcement KIND (dialog._sdtPhaseKind/_sdtPhaseSeenAt, beside _sdtAnnounced's own per-dialog bookkeeping) and reports the age of that observation in its own node, sdt-switch-age. Keyed on kind rather than phase so an idle count moving from 4 to 5 does not reset an age that has been accumulating. Both rejected designs are named in the code comment so neither returns: a phaseSince on the scheduler lies, because the abort path deliberately leaves phase stale at extracting; and the journal's 60 s heartbeat already records phase with elapsed, so a second copy has an owner problem. SDT_PHASE_AGE_THRESHOLD_MS = 60000, and the mutant WAS RUN, twice, which found a defect in the first version of the test: arms written as SDT_PHASE_AGE_THRESHOLD_MS - 1 MOVE WITH a mutated constant, and 60001 survived them -- only the pinned-value assertion was red. Rewritten as literals, 59999 and 60000, and re-run: 59999 is caught by 'an age one millisecond under the threshold is shown' and 60001 by 'an age exactly at the threshold is withheld', each with the pinned-value assertion temporarily removed so the behavioural arm is shown to catch it alone. The age never reaches the announcement channel: describeSDTPauseControl's kind stays digits-free and announceSDTTransition is handed the line without it, asserted both in the dialog suite and by a source-level test.
