in, which is what that file describes. Writing 0.4.26 into it before this PR merges would be the false move, and the post-merge refresh is /roar's step. Independently confirmed by the reviewer on its own run rather than relayed: make check exit 0, 1462 passed 15 skipped, bootstrap mutants 91/91 with M49f new and caught, scheduler 21/21. Also recorded from the round: the 'vanished' fix was positive-controlled three times independently, three reviewers reverting it and getting three reds, which is the strongest evidence in either round.
--- body ---

## Context

Author, 2026-09-15, having asked whether a ticket existed for this and been
shown 0792's deferred section:

> J'en ai marre du bouton. C'est un checkbox "Pause indexing". Qui commence
> checked, et devient controlable quand l'indexing peut être lancé (boot wait,
> sync). Quand il est auto-checked, ça dit pourquoi. Quand il est unchecked, ça
> dit la phase.

### What the ruling fixes

`#sdt-switch` is a `<button>` whose visible text names the **action to take** --
"Turn indexing on" / "Turn indexing off" -- so it inverts in the same instant it
is clicked. Ticket 0790 patched the *announcement* (an `aria-label` carrying a
state-then-action sentence, `switch-turned-on` / `switch-turned-off`) and left
the widget alone, because the widget was the open question 0792 then deferred.

A checkbox carries state natively. Checked/unchecked *is* the state, the label
never inverts, and the 0790 `aria-label` workaround can go with it: the reason
that attribute exists is that a button's name had to do a job a checkbox's
`checked` does for free.

It also puts the UI on SPEC.md's own vocabulary. R22 is named **"Pause"**; the
control that satisfies it has until now said "Turn indexing off".

### Two corrections this ruling makes to closed ticket 0792

1. **Pause/Play was rejected there; the label is now the author's.** 0792's
   ground was that `launch-worker` "cannot be paused once a file has started",
   and that pause implies session-scoped state where this is a remembered
   preference. The first half becomes a **design constraint on one string, not
   a veto**: checking the box ends admissions and the document already in
   flight finishes, exactly as `disarmSDTSitter` already behaves ("nothing is
   cancelled; the sitter simply stops asking"). The line beside the box must
   say so while it is true, and `RELEASE-NOTES.md` must not promise an instant
   stop. No scheduler or admission semantics change anywhere in this ticket.
   The second half is answered by the reading adopted below: the preference
   stays remembered.

2. **Drop `role="switch"`.** 0792's recommendation was
   `<input type="checkbox" role="switch">`, chosen when the control was still
   imagined as a slider. With "Pause indexing" as the label it is a checkbox;
   the ARIA role would only make a screen reader say "switch" about a thing the
   label calls a checkbox. A plain `<input type="checkbox">` sits strictly
   inside 0792's costed option 1: no Lit, no `chrome://` coupling, no
   stylesheet, no `<style>` element in a window built from twelve inline
   `style.cssText` sites, and Gecko keeps the native widget, the focus ring and
   the forced-colours rendering.

### Settled: no remembered state, start on as soon as possible

Author, 2026-09-15, asked which of the two readings below he meant and
answering with a third:

> Je préfère ne pas avoir d'état donc aucune préférence mémorisée. D'ailleurs
> ce n'est marqué nulle part "Préférence mémorisée". On commence allumé dès que
> possible.

Neither reading this ticket first offered, then. There is no remembered answer
to survive or to revoke. The box is checked and non-interactive while indexing
cannot be launched, the line says why, and the moment it can be launched
**indexing starts**: the box unchecks itself and nobody is asked. Checking it
afterwards pauses for this session, and the next Zotero start begins indexing
again.

The two readings that are now moot, kept in one line each so nobody re-derives
them: the pref survives and the box reflects it (what this ticket first
adopted), or the box starts checked every session and the user unchecks it each
time (which would have revoked ticket 0742). The ruling discards the mechanism
both were arguing about.

**His second sentence, checked.** It is very nearly true, and the exception
argues for the ruling rather than against it. Exactly one string says the
choice persists -- `launch-details`, "This answer is remembered." -- and it
lives in the launch modal, which is shown at most once per profile and
deliberately never returns (ticket 0742). So the only statement that the
preference is remembered sits on the one surface designed never to be seen
twice, while the window the user actually consults says nothing about it. State
that nothing on screen admits to is the thing the ruling removes.

### R22: the durable half is the host's, the session half is ours

Author, 2026-09-15, on being shown that a session-scoped pause contradicts
R22's second clause:

> Zotero a un bouton pour désactiver un plugin.

That is the answer, and it is better than weakening the requirement. R22 asks
for one obvious way to stop all background work, holding across restarts. The
two clauses are now answered by two controls, one of which the plugin does not
have to build:

- **Pause, for this session** -- the checkbox, one click, in the window the
  user already has open.
- **Stop, durably** -- Zotero's own add-on disable. It holds across restarts by
  construction, and re-implementing a durable stop beside it was the plugin
  storing state the host already stores.

So R22 is not weakened. What SPEC.md owes is a sentence saying which control
answers which clause, because today the text reads as though one switch had to
do both.

**What that sentence must answer, and must not pretend is new.** Ticket 0742 --
titled, in as many words, "Collapse the sitter's launch prompt and pause into
one persisted switch" -- considered add-on disable for exactly this role and
rejected it. `bootstrap.js:1039-1042` still carries the reasoning: disable
"held across restarts but lived four clicks away in Tools -> Add-ons and
removed the very window that would have shown the sitter stopped". Both
observations are still true. What changed is their weight, and the SPEC edit
has to say so rather than quietly drop them: they were fatal while disable was
the ONLY durable stop and the routine need -- quiet it, now -- had to travel
through it. With a one-click pause in the window, reaching for the durable stop
becomes a rare, deliberate act, and a rare deliberate act may cost four clicks
and may take the window with it. Removing the UI is the correct behaviour for
someone who meant to stop it for good.

**Say plainly that this reverses 0742.** Its launch prompt goes (Action 3b),
its persistence goes, and the durable half returns to the host. What survives
is the half that worked: one control, in the window, and "paused" as a state
the user can see and leave rather than the silence a removed UI leaves behind.

### Which states check the box by themselves

The ruling names boot wait and sync as the conditions that hold the control.
Read with "when it is unchecked, it says the phase", that puts the blocked
phases on the *unchecked* side: `cpu-busy`, `low-memory`, `low-disk`,
`storage-unavailable` and `resources-unavailable` are phases, they already have
decided labels in `SDT_PHASE_LABELS`, and the user has not paused anything when
one of them holds. Auto-checked is reserved for the two states where the user's
intent cannot change the outcome at all.

**CORRECTION, made during implementation: `native-worker-busy` is NOT one of the
blocked phases**, and this paragraph listed it as a sixth. Verified on
`origin/main`: `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` is `['cpu-busy', 'low-memory', 'low-disk',
'storage-unavailable', 'resources-unavailable', SDT_SYNC_PHASE]` and
`native-worker-busy` is not in it. The difference is the retry cadence rather
than the wording -- `nextSweepDelayMS` gives blocked phases the ten-minute idle
interval and gives this one the ordinary sweep interval, and scheduler.js emits
it at `trace` as `worker-idle-wait` rather than as a `refuse`. So it is a WAIT,
not a block, and the table's "a blocked phase" row did not cover it.

It belongs on the same row all the same, for the reasons that row exists rather
than for membership in a list: the user has paused nothing, it is transient, and
it already has a decided `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` entry ("Waiting: native indexing
under way"). Auto-checking a phase that turns over in seconds would flicker the
control exactly as `cpu-busy` would. The row is therefore read as *any phase
with a decided label that is neither the user's own pause nor the sync*, which
is what the implementation does -- it falls through, and nothing names it.

The general form of the error is worth keeping: two classifications that agree
on five of six members are the ones that get conflated. `SDT_SYNC_PHASE` is the
other direction of the same trap -- it IS in `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES` and belongs on
the *checked* row -- so neither membership nor non-membership decides this
table, and a composer that reached for `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES.includes` would be
right for five phases and wrong for two.

Keeping a transient `cpu-busy` off the checkbox also keeps the control from
flickering checked and unchecked under ordinary load, which is the 10 Hz defect
in slower clothes -- the thing 0792's own invariants exist to prevent.

### The table the implementation owes

| Sitter state | box | interactive | the line beside it says |
|---|---|---|---|
| boot wait -- the sitter cannot yet be armed | checked | no | why: Zotero is still starting |
| sync in progress | checked | no | why: waiting for sync to finish |
| user paused this session (`phase === 'switched-off'`) | checked | yes | `switch-off`, minus any claim of persistence |
| checked, a document still in flight | checked | yes | `switch-off-in-flight` -- see the correction below; NOT "finishing the current document, then stopping" |
| `census` | unchecked | yes | `switch-on-census` |
| `draining` | unchecked | yes | `switch-on-draining` |
| `extracting` | unchecked | yes | `switch-on-extracting` |
| a blocked phase, or the `native-worker-busy` wait | unchecked | yes | that phase's `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` entry |
| `error` | unchecked | yes | `switch-on-error`, which the phase already has (`bootstrap.js:1665`) and which this table omitted until the review caught it |
| idle | unchecked | yes | `describeSDTIdleLine`'s counts |
| removal noticed (0781) | checked | no | `vanished-message`, unchanged |

Two rows are new work and not merely a rewiring of what exists: **sync** has no
gate anywhere in the plugin today (see Actions 4), and **checked with a document
in flight** has no string today.

**CORRECTION to the in-flight row, made during implementation, and it is a
correction to what the row PROMISES rather than to its wording.** "Finishing the
current document, then stopping" is false, and now established rather than
suspected. A submitted extraction cannot be ended from the sitter's side at all:
`ensure()`'s whole option surface is `isPriority` and `onProgress`, the worker's
dispatcher has no cancel action, and `Zotero.PDFWorker._worker.terminate()` is
reachable but strictly worse than doing nothing -- the parked promise never
settles, `_processingQueue` never resets, and the queue shared with full-text
indexing never drains again for the life of the process. From this side a slow
tail and a wedged worker leave the same journal (DESIGN-NOTES open question 4,
ticket 0793's log). So the sentence cannot promise a stop, and it must be true
of the wedged case as well as the ordinary one.

What ships says what is true and names the recourse that works, which is the
host's: "Indexing is paused. No new attachment is picked up. One was already
handed to Zotero's shared extractor before you paused, and the assistant has no
way to end it: it finishes, or stops, on Zotero's own terms. To stop everything
now, disable the assistant in Zotero's Add-ons window." That is R22's durable
clause doing the work its own ruling assigns it.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js`
  - `~2639-2660` -- where the control is built: `sdt-switch-row`, the
    `sdt-switch-state` span, and `element('button', 'sdt-switch')` with its
    `type=button`, its `white-space: nowrap` sizing note and its click listener.
    The flex `space-between` comment explains why the button is pinned right;
    a checkbox's box + label is a different shape and that reasoning must be
    re-read, not inherited.
  - `~2322-2356` -- the per-render block: `#sdt-switch-state`'s text,
    `announceSDTTransition`, `switch-turn-on`/`switch-turn-off` and the 0790
    `aria-label`. `const off = s.phase === 'switched-off'` is the polarity that
    inverts here: **checked <=> not indexing**.
  - `~1651-1660` `describeSDTSwitchLine` -- already says the phase when on and
    `switch-off` when off; it gains the two new cases.
  - `~1672-1690` `describeSDTSwitchKind` -- the announcement identity. It must
    stay digits-free, or a moving count reaches the live region.
  - `~1014-1035` `SDT_PHASE_LABELS`, `~718` `SDT_BLOCKED_PHASES`.
  - `~1052-1070` `readSDTSwitch` / `writeSDTSwitch`; `~1140-1150`
    `toggleSDTSwitch` (writes the pref FIRST, deliberately).
  - `~1104-1135` `armSDTSitter` / `disarmSDTSitter`.
  - `~3436-3459` -- the startup launch decision, where `enabled === null`
    reaches `askSDTLaunch` and where `armSDTSitter()` or `disarmSDTSitter()` is
    chosen. This is where "boot wait" has to become a state the render can read
    rather than a gap before `alive = true`.
  - `~354-373` -- the string table: `switch-on-prefix`, `switch-off`,
    `switch-turn-on`, `switch-turn-off`, `switch-turned-on`, `switch-turned-off`.
- `tests/sdt_sitter_dialog.mjs` -- the dialog suite, drives a stub document and
  pins the window's layer order.
- `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs`, `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` -- the
  scheduler suite asks `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` about every phase it can enumerate.
- `verification/probes/sdt-sitter-harness/bootstrap.js` -- already asserts a
  bound checkbox label for the diagnostics pref; the same pattern applies.
- `verification/probes/sdt_panel_keyboard.js`, `run_panel_keyboard.py` -- focus
  and keyboard assertions.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/RELEASE-NOTES.md` and `DESIGN-NOTES.md`.
- `SPEC.md` **at the repository root** -- R22 and its sitter passage
  (~2246-2266). There is no `plugins/sdt-sitter/SPEC.md`; an earlier draft of
  this ticket cited one that does not exist.
- `tests/test_sdt_sitter.py` -- the Python side, and the one Action 3b will
  redden: it carries ~15 assertions over the launch prompt and the enabled
  pref, and `make check-fast` runs it. Removing the modal without touching this
  file turns a green branch red at the first gate that reads it.
- `verification/SDT-SITTER-LAUNCH.md` -- the launch flow's own document. It
  describes the prompt and the persisted answer that Action 3b removes.

## Actions

1. Replace `element('button', 'sdt-switch')` with an `<input type="checkbox">`
   plus a bound `<label>`, unstyled, no `role`. Bind by `for`/`id` so the label
   text is clickable and is the accessible name. Re-read the row's flex
   reasoning rather than porting it: the comment that pins the button right was
   written about a two-word button whose text changes length, and neither
   clause is true of a fixed "Pause indexing" label.
2. Invert the polarity at the one place it is computed. `checked` is
   `!indexing`, derived from the sitter's phase exactly as `off` is today --
   "reread from the sitter's own phase rather than from the pref: the two
   agree, and a disagreement shows here instead of hiding" is a property worth
   keeping.
3. Make boot wait a readable state. Today the window does not exist before
   `alive = true`, so "checked and non-interactive during boot wait" needs the
   render to be able to distinguish *not yet armable* from *user paused*. Do
   not invent a phase name that announces per document.
3b. Remove the persisted answer, all of it or none of it. `readSDTSwitch` stops
   being consulted at startup (`bootstrap.js:3436`) and the startup path arms
   unconditionally once indexing is launchable; `askSDTLaunch` goes with it,
   because a prompt whose only job is to write a preference that no longer
   exists has nothing to ask. Half of this is worse than none: dropping the
   modal while still reading the pref leaves a machine that obeys an answer the
   user can no longer give.
   Three traps in that removal, all three read in the source rather than
   assumed:
   - **Do not sweep the `launch-` prefix.** The modal owns exactly six ids --
     `launch-title`, `launch-question`, `launch-conditions`, `launch-details`,
     `launch-yes`, `launch-no`. `launch-worker` and `launch-disable` are NOT
     modal strings: `bootstrap.js:2019` feeds them, with `about-updates`, to the
     About panel's disclosures. They are the paragraphs explaining that
     extraction runs on a shared background process, and that turning indexing
     off stops new attachments while one already being processed finishes --
     the two things a user who has just ticked "Pause indexing" is most likely
     to go looking for. A prefix sweep would empty the About panel of exactly
     the text this ticket's own label makes relevant.
   - **`writeSDTSwitch` is not only a pref write.** It also calls
     `emit('switch', { enabled })` (`bootstrap.js:1065`), and those ring records
     are what let a post-mortem reconstruct the control's transitions -- they
     are how the 2026-09-15 evening's two cycles and their `completed: 0` were
     measured at all. If the pref write goes with the function, the emission
     must survive it, or the diagnostics lose the only trace the control leaves.
   - **The tri-state is also a safety guard, and dissolving it is a decision.**
     `readSDTSwitch`'s `null` does not only mean "never answered": the `catch`
     returns `null` for an UNREADABLE pref too, and `bootstrap.js:1049-1051`
     says why that conflation is deliberate -- "asking a question that was
     already answered is a nuisance, where silently indexing on a machine whose
     answer could not be read is the thing the question exists to prevent."
     Starting on unconditionally removes that protection along with the memory.
     Under this ruling it has nothing left to protect, since indexing without
     being asked is now the intended default -- but say so in the commit. It
     must be decided, not carried away.
   Decide explicitly what happens to `extensions.sdt-pack-sitter.enabled`
   itself -- a pref left written but unread is exactly the invisible state the
   ruling is against -- and to the uninstall write -- `if (named === 'uninstall' &&
   readSDTSwitch() !== null) writeSDTSwitch(false);`, `bootstrap.js:3569` as of
   `c7c7c3a6`. That line has been cited as 3548, 3560 and 3569 within one
   evening by three readers on three trees: quote the statement, and treat the
   number as perishable.
4. Add the sync condition. **No Zotero-sync gate exists in the plugin today** --
   grep for it before building: `sync` appears in `bootstrap.js` only in a
   not-indexed detail string and in unrelated prose. The candidate reading,
   from the concurrent session's live work on tickets 0795/0796, is
   `Zotero.Sync.Runner.syncInProgress`. **It is a candidate, not a fact**: it
   must be sampled twice, once while a sync is running and once at rest, and
   adopted only if the two readings differ. A key identical in both states does
   not discriminate and must not be used -- that probe is exactly this ticket's
   positive control, and the reading is taken from a live Zotero window, since
   nothing offline can settle it.
5. Retire `switch-turn-on` / `switch-turn-off` / `switch-turned-on` /
   `switch-turned-off` and add: the checkbox label, the boot-wait reason, the
   sync reason, and the in-flight "finishing the current document" line. Delete
   the retired ids rather than leaving them unreferenced.
6. Drop the 0790 `aria-label` on this control and say in the commit why it is
   safe to drop: the defect it patched was a button label that named the
   inverse action, and a checkbox has no such label.
7. Update `RELEASE-NOTES.md` and `DESIGN-NOTES.md`. The release note must state
   that checking the box stops new work and lets the current document finish --
   the promise 0792 refused to let Pause/Play make.
8. Bump the manifest to 0.4.18 or later. PR #585 already holds 0.4.17, and
   `bench/check_sitter_version.py` rule 3 reddens when two builds answer to one
   number. Two mechanics of that guard, reported by the session that just hit
   both and confirmed here against the script:
   - **It hashes bytes, not behaviour.** The payload is digested with
     `hashlib.sha256` over the files `build_sdt_sitter.DELIVERED` names, and the
     invariant is that no two DIFFERENT payloads share a version. A
     comment-only edit to `bootstrap.js` is a different payload. Expect to bump
     for a change that alters nothing at runtime, and do not read a redden here
     as evidence that behaviour moved.
   - **NOT-RUN is not green.** The forge leg reports on its own line and answers
     NOT-RUN when it cannot enumerate the open pull requests -- an HTTP 504 did
     it once -- because "a gate that cannot look must never answer as" green
     (its own words, `check_sitter_version.py:85`). A version another open
     branch has already claimed would read as unclaimed in that state. Re-run
     it; never take the NOT-RUN line for a pass.
9. Rewrite SPEC.md's R22 passage for the sitter, in the same PR as the
   behaviour. **It is a passage, not a sentence, and it asserts the reversed
   design as fact.** `SPEC.md` at the REPO ROOT (not `plugins/sdt-sitter/`,
   which has no SPEC.md), around lines 2246-2266 as of `c7c7c3a6`, currently
   says: "A preference records the user's answer; the question is asked once
   per profile, on the first activation that finds it unanswered, and never
   again, so the answer holds across a restart and across a disable and
   re-enable." Every clause of that is what this ticket removes.
   One sentence in it is the exact inverse of the author's ruling and must be
   reversed, not softened: "Zotero's plugin disable control keeps its graceful
   host-level semantics and **is no longer R22's control for the sitter**."
   The ruling of 2026-09-15 makes it R22's control for the durable clause
   again. A SPEC that keeps this sentence while the code ships the ruling is
   not a stale doc, it is a denial of the design.
   Carry forward, rather than silently drop, ticket 0742's two objections to
   add-on disable (four clicks away; it removes the window that would have
   shown the sitter stopped) and say why they no longer decide the question.
   Also in the same commit, the FOUR sites in `bootstrap.js` that cite R22 --
   line numbers as of `c7c7c3a6`, and re-locate them by symbol rather than by
   number, because #585 moved every one of them tonight:
   - `~114-119`, the `ENABLED_PREF` declaration: "Tri-state: unset means the
     question has never been answered ... both hold across a restart and
     across a disable/re-enable, which is what R22 requires".
   - `~1038`, opening "the switch: R22's one obvious way (ticket 0742)".
   - `~2656`, on why the control leads the window: "R22's 'one obvious way'".
   - `~3650`, in `removeSDTDurableState`, contrasting the debug pref with
     "R22's tri-state discipline for ENABLED_PREF above" -- retiring the
     tri-state leaves it pointing at a clause that no longer exists.
   Do not treat this list as closed: `grep -n R22 plugins/sdt-sitter/*.js
   SPEC.md verification/SDT-SITTER-LAUNCH.md` is the sweep, and the Verification
   section makes it a checked step rather than a claim.

10. **Show how long the window has been saying the same thing.** ADDED AFTER
   FILING, and its provenance is written out here rather than left implicit,
   because the review of PR 596 was right that it appeared in no Action, no
   Context and no exit criterion while every other decision in this ticket
   carries a verbatim author quote.

   **This one does not, and that is the honest state of it.** It reached the
   implementation as a relayed instruction in the task brief that dispatched the
   work, not as a quoted message: the author wants to see how long the sitter has
   held its current phase, because on 2026-09-15 his window showed a plausible
   steady state while `draining` had held for eight minutes. The observation
   behind it is his and is dated; the wording of the requirement is the relayer's.
   A relayed claim is unverified until someone measures it or names whose
   measurement it is, so it is named here.

   Two designs are ruled OUT in the same instruction, and both reasons are
   checkable in this repository rather than taken on trust:
   - a `phaseSince` field on the scheduler LIES, because the abort path
     deliberately leaves `phase` stale at `extracting`;
   - the journal already records phase with elapsed every 60 s, so a second copy
     has an owner problem.

   What ships is therefore a **render-local observation**: the dialog notes when
   it first saw the current announcement kind and reports the age of that
   observation. It is honest about what it measures, needs no scheduler change,
   and cannot go stale behind an abort. Two constraints: the age must not reach
   the announcement channel (`describeSDTSwitchKind` stays digits-free, or a
   duration is spoken to a screen reader as though the machine had changed
   state), and a threshold decides when the age is worth showing -- "2 s ago" is
   noise, "8 min" is the diagnosis. That threshold is a number that decides
   something, so it gets an assertion on its exact value AND on one unit either
   side of it, and the mutants get run.

   **If the author disowns it, it lifts out cleanly** -- and the first version of
   this sentence said "one constant, one observer, one composer, one `<span>`,
   and the two tests", which the review of PR 596 measured and found wrong by
   about half. It is the reversibility argument for shipping a feature the author
   has not ratified, so its accuracy is load-bearing, and the honest figure comes
   from the grep rather than from memory:

       grep -rn "SDT_PHASE_AGE_THRESHOLD_MS\|observeSDTPhaseAge\|\
       describeSDTPhaseAge\|sdt-switch-age\|switch-age\|_sdtPhaseSeenAt\|\
       _sdtPhaseKind" plugins/ tests/

   **Three files, and nothing outside them**: 14 lines in
   `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` (the constant, `observeSDTPhaseAge`,
   `describeSDTPhaseAge`, the `switch-age` string-table entry, the
   `sdt-switch-age` node built in `populate`, and the two call-site lines in
   `renderSDTPauseRow` -- deleting the functions without those is a
   `ReferenceError`, which is the half the first count missed), 11 in
   `tests/sdt_sitter_dialog.mjs` across THREE tests rather than two, and 7 in
   `tests/test_sdt_sitter.py` in one test. Plus one `RELEASE-NOTES.md` paragraph.

   Still a clean lift -- no other reading in the window consumes it, no
   scheduler state backs it, and `describeSDTPauseControl` does not know it
   exists -- but it is a dozen and a half lines across four files, not five
   things.

## Test

Red first, in `tests/sdt_sitter_dialog.mjs`, against the stub document:

- The control is an `input[type=checkbox]` with a bound label, and its
  `checked` is true when the phase is `switched-off` and false when the phase
  is `census`. Today there is no checkbox at all, so this is red by absence.
- **The control that matters**: assert the box is `disabled` during boot wait
  and `enabled` under a blocked phase (`cpu-busy`). A test that only asserts
  "checked when off" passes against a checkbox wired to the pref alone and
  would miss the entire ruling. Both arms must be shown to fail before the fix
  -- a suite where the blocked-phase arm is green by construction is the defect
  ticket 0787 recorded in its own words: focusable and reachable were never the
  same claim.
- The announcement kind stays digits-free across a count change.

